# Profile invite links. /u/<username> is one static page that fills the handle in from the URL;
# there is no per-person file and nothing is looked up. 200 rewrites rather than redirects, so the
# address the recipient was sent is the address they keep.
#
# Deliberately NOT a catch-all: on Cloudflare a matching rule wins even when a real file exists at
# the path, the opposite of Netlify, so a broad pattern here would shadow real pages and the
# /.well-known files that universal links depend on.
# The target is invite.html, NOT u.html, and the name matters. Cloudflare exposes a top-level
# HTML file at its extensionless path, so a u.html here becomes /u, and it then normalises
# /u/<username> to /u with a 308 BEFORE this rule is consulted - which returns 200 and looks
# fine while silently stripping the username the page exists to read. Verified on the live
# domain, not locally: a local server has no such normalisation, so this cannot be caught
# before deploying.
/u/*    /invite    200

